## Key Commands

```bash
{% if dev_in_worktrees %}
# A worktree env per pipeline run: start (idempotent), list, stop; --env-repo adds this repo's
# own worktree on branch <slug> at .worktrees/_envrepo/<slug> (ticket scripts, ADRs, skills)
bash .claude/skills/worktree-env/scripts/wt-start.sh <slug> [--env-repo]
bash .claude/skills/worktree-env/scripts/wt-list.sh
bash .claude/skills/worktree-env/scripts/wt-stop.sh <slug>

# Services: the postgres cluster, and one Odoo per session
systemctl --user {start|stop|restart|status} postgres{{ service_suffix }}.service odoo{{ service_suffix }}-wt@<slug>.service

# Update/install module in a session (stop its odoo first)
{{ odoo_cmd }} -c ${{ project_dir_var }}/.worktrees/_env/<slug>/odoo.conf -u module_name --workers 0 --stop-after-init --logfile=

# Interactive shell on a session
{{ odoo_cmd }} shell -c ${{ project_dir_var }}/.worktrees/_env/<slug>/odoo.conf --workers 0 --no-http

# Run a specific test class/method in a session (its conf and DB, a free port, the
# IPv4 loopback HttpCase needs, a dbfilter naming the session DB). Selector:
# /module_name[:TestClass[.test_method]]; without the leading / it is a tag and runs 0 tests
bash .claude/skills/worktree-env/scripts/wt-test.sh <slug> -u module_name --test-enable --test-tags /module_name:TestClassName
{% else %}
# Services
systemctl --user {start|stop|restart|status} odoo{{ service_suffix }}.service postgres{{ service_suffix }}.service nginx{{ service_suffix }}.service

# Update/install module (stop running odoo first)
{{ odoo_cmd }} -c ${{ project_dir_var }}/odoo.conf -u module_name --workers 0 --stop-after-init --logfile=

# Interactive shell
{{ odoo_cmd }} shell -c ${{ project_dir_var }}/odoo.conf --workers 0 --stop-after-init

# Run a specific test class/method. Selector: /module_name[:TestClass[.test_method]];
# without the leading / it is a tag and runs 0 tests
{{ odoo_cmd }} -c ${{ project_dir_var }}/odoo.conf -u module_name --workers 0 --test-enable --test-tags /module_name:TestClassName --stop-after-init --logfile=
{% endif %}

# Clone/update repos and regenerate addon symlinks
nix run .#update-repos
```

## Architecture

```
src/
├── odoo/                  # Odoo {{ odoo_version }} CE source (read-only, branch {{ odoo_version }})
{% if custom_repo_name %}
├── {{ custom_repo_name }}/   # Custom modules — this is where we develop
{% endif %}
└── ...                    # Third-party repos from addons.yaml (read-only)
```

{% if custom_repo_name %}
- **Custom modules** live in `src/{{ custom_repo_name }}/` (prefix `{{ module_prefix }}_*`)
{% endif %}
- **Third-party repos** are pinned in `addons.yaml`
- **Addon symlinks** are managed at `.local/share/Odoo/addons/{{ odoo_version }}/` by `nix run .#update-repos`
- **Before typing `grep -r` over `src/`**: if the pattern is an identifier — method, field, model name, XML id — that is a `find-code` question, not a grep (`python3 .claude/skills/find-code/lsp.py sym|def|refs|model`). It is MRO-aware and answers over the whole tree at once, while a hand-scoped grep silently misses the repos you did not list in the command. grep stays right for plain strings: comments, log lines, data files, message text
- **Never pipe a "who uses X" search into `head`/`tail`.** *Who consumes this identifier* is only useful answered in full: a truncated list reads as "these are the call sites", so the conclusion drawn from it is wrong rather than merely partial. Output too long to read is the signal to ask `lsp.py refs`, not to cut it. Field report: `grep -rn leave_rendered ... | head -20` hid the two references that decided the answer, and the wrong conclusion reached a ticket before being caught. The find-code nudge hook flags this case even when its session budget is spent
- Never edit OCA/core code under `src/` (enforced by the guard-readonly hook){% if custom_repo_name %} — extend via a `{{ module_prefix }}_*` module{% endif %}
{% if use_kb %}


## Memory: kb

Memory here lives in **kb**, a Neo4j graph (<https://github.com/okolovmark/kb>){% if use_claude_code %}, not in Claude Code's auto-memory directory: keep that off for this project (`"autoMemoryEnabled": false` in `.claude/settings.local.json`){% endif %}. kb is user-level (one install and one database for every project); `nix run .#kb-setup` installs it when missing and registers this checkout as its scope. Until then the session start says so in one line, and this section does not apply.

- **Session start**: `.claude/hooks/kb-session-start.sh` opens the session record and prints the pinned rules and a one-line standup count, under the 10,000 characters Claude Code keeps of a hook's output. The rest is a command away: `kb today --scope {{ project_name }}` lists the standup (the user sees it in the kb band and the `/kb` pane), `kb index --scope {{ project_name }}` the recent records; notices (`kb add --once`) are the user's and show in the `/kb` pane. No kb text in context → `kb index --scope {{ project_name }}` and `kb today --scope {{ project_name }}`; `kb: NO MEMORY` means Neo4j did not come up: say so first, then `kb status` and `journalctl --user -u neo4j-kb.service -n 100`. After a compaction the hook asks for a `kb session note` first
- **Ground rules** are pinned feedback records: `kb index --scope {{ project_name }}` lists them, `kb show <slug>` reads one, `kb search "<topic>"` finds one. A kb record is a dated fact about this environment; Odoo framework knowledge stays in the skills
- **Read**: `kb show <id|slug>` · `kb search "<text>"` · `kb links <id>` · `kb why <id>` · `kb next --scope {{ project_name }}`
- **Tasks are the user's call**: work found outside the task at hand (an unrelated fix, a follow-up, a check for later, a non-blocking review finding) is never filed or fixed unasked. Ask first, one AskUserQuestion question per item: here (this session), a kb task{% if tickets_mcp == 'odoo' %}, an Odoo ticket{% endif %}, or skip. Data errors and wishes the user has not taken on are said once and dropped; drift your own change caused is fixed on the spot. `.claude/hooks/guard-kb-task.py` refuses `kb add` of a task without `# user-picked-kb` in the command
- **Write**: a task, open thread or blocker the user picked kb for (or asked for): `kb add "<title>" --scope {{ project_name }} --tag <area>  # user-picked-kb` (`--due D`, `--plan D`, `--window weekend`, `--once`; no flag means it ages). A fact: `kb add "<title>" --kind note|howto|feedback|reference|decision --summary "<one line>" --body-file <f> --scope {{ project_name }}|global|personal --tag <area>`. Every record but a session or a personal one needs an area tag: `kb tags` lists the vocabulary, the project's areas live in `.kb/tags.toml` (a new one goes in through a PR). A record that states a rule for a kind of work also carries that type tag (`--tag migration`, `data_fix`; `kb tags` lists the types), or `kb context` never pulls it into another module's task. `kb context <id>` lists what kb knows about a task's areas and types. Change: `kb append|edit|done|skip|snooze|archive <id>`, `kb link <a> RELATED <b>`
{% if tickets_mcp == 'odoo' %}
- **Tickets**: {{ ticket_prefix }} tasks arrive through `kb sync odoo`, which the hook runs. Never create one by hand; `kb promote <id> {{ ticket_prefix }}-xxxx` ties a thread to a ticket the `odoo-tickets` skill created
{% endif %}
- **This session**: `kb session note "<text>"` as you go; before signing off on substantive work, `kb session close --body-file <f>` with Intent, Tags, Files touched, Done, Decisions, Open threads
- **Scope** comes from the working directory: this project plus `global`; `--scope personal` only when the user asks
{% endif %}
